1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,635
At six forty-four this morning,
something closed around the Sun.

2
00:00:05,405 --> 00:00:08,420
Its last light is still racing toward you.

3
00:00:08,890 --> 00:00:12,125
It will reach Earth in eight
minutes and twenty seconds.

4
00:00:12,595 --> 00:00:16,080
So for eight more minutes, it is
just a summer morning.

5
00:00:16,550 --> 00:00:20,115
Your little sister Mira is
drawing the Sun. Again.

6
00:00:20,585 --> 00:00:22,720
Biscuit is planning a crime.

7
00:00:23,190 --> 00:00:26,650
Somewhere, someone catches the
best wave of their life.

8
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:29,585
A farmer checks the sky for rain.

9
00:00:30,055 --> 00:00:32,890
A kid misses the bus by one second.

10
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:36,095
And right behind the light comes the dark.

11
00:00:36,565 --> 00:00:38,105
Three. Two.

12
00:00:38,575 --> 00:00:39,590
One.

13
00:00:40,975 --> 00:00:42,285
MIRA: Why is it night?

14
00:00:46,555 --> 00:00:49,265
Every phone on the street is pointing up.

15
00:00:49,735 --> 00:00:52,170
Someone shouts that it is an eclipse.

16
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:55,300
Every network jams in the same minute.

17
00:00:55,770 --> 00:00:59,860
But eclipses last minutes. And
the Moon has gone dark too.

18
00:01:00,330 --> 00:01:03,390
Its light was only ever
borrowed from the Sun.

19
00:01:03,860 --> 00:01:06,375
Then someone hammers on your door.

20
00:01:06,845 --> 00:01:08,935
MR. ITO: You. Come to the roof.

21
00:01:09,405 --> 00:01:12,020
Bring the girl. And the dog.

22
00:01:12,490 --> 00:01:18,055
Mr. Ito taught astronomy for forty years.
He has never been this excited.

23
00:01:18,525 --> 00:01:21,465
MR. ITO: Look. Venus is still shining.

24
00:01:21,935 --> 00:01:23,575
MIRA: But the Sun is gone!

25
00:01:24,045 --> 00:01:26,360
MR. ITO: Venus is a mirror.

26
00:01:26,830 --> 00:01:30,695
The light it shows us left
the Sun minutes ago.

27
00:01:31,165 --> 00:01:33,950
Three minutes later, Venus goes out.

28
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:40,285
MR. ITO: If something blocked the Sun close to
us, everything would go dark at once.

29
00:01:40,755 --> 00:01:43,170
This happened at the Sun.

30
00:01:43,940 --> 00:01:49,380
Jupiter is much further out. It should
go dark in seventy-two minutes.

31
00:01:49,850 --> 00:01:52,135
So you sit down, and you wait.

32
00:01:52,850 --> 00:01:56,315
Seventy-two minutes later, to
the second, it does.

33
00:01:59,385 --> 00:02:01,200
Then you look at your watch.

34
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:05,080
Without the Sun, Earth is
losing heat to space.

35
00:02:05,550 --> 00:02:07,540
A little more every hour.

36
00:02:08,010 --> 00:02:11,275
Day one. The whole city has the same idea.

37
00:02:11,745 --> 00:02:14,485
Candles. Rice. Batteries. Water.

38
00:02:14,955 --> 00:02:16,845
Mira has her own list.

39
00:02:17,315 --> 00:02:20,050
The last heater in the
shop starts a fight.

40
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:23,380
Back home, you pick one room and seal it.

41
00:02:23,850 --> 00:02:27,335
Mr. Ito moves in with you. He lives alone.

42
00:02:27,805 --> 00:02:31,590
Every person gives off about a
hundred watts of heat.

43
00:02:32,060 --> 00:02:35,550
Congratulations. You are
all radiators now.

44
00:02:36,020 --> 00:02:37,985
Then you fill the bathtub.

45
00:02:38,455 --> 00:02:42,170
When the pipes freeze, this is all
the water you will have.

46
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:46,875
You will not need the freezer. The
whole planet is becoming one.

47
00:02:47,345 --> 00:02:52,485
(RADIO) Harbour officials say this
morning’s high tide was completely normal.

48
00:02:52,955 --> 00:02:55,670
MR. ITO: Normal! The tide was normal!

49
00:02:56,140 --> 00:02:56,980
MIRA: So?

50
00:02:58,140 --> 00:03:01,680
MR. ITO: The Moon pulls the
sea. So does the Sun.

51
00:03:02,150 --> 00:03:06,940
Without the Sun, this week’s big
tides would be a third smaller.

52
00:03:07,410 --> 00:03:10,900
Watch. The string keeps
the key going round.

53
00:03:11,370 --> 00:03:14,435
Cut the string, and it flies away.

54
00:03:14,905 --> 00:03:18,720
We have not flown away. We
are still going round.

55
00:03:19,190 --> 00:03:21,630
The Sun is still there.

56
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:25,015
Something is in front of it.

57
00:03:28,385 --> 00:03:34,225
Ito radios every observatory he knows. By
night, the whole world has heard.

58
00:03:34,695 --> 00:03:39,480
Day three. Ito says one last thing is
still on its way from the Sun.

59
00:03:39,950 --> 00:03:41,790
MR. ITO: Look! Up there!

60
00:03:42,260 --> 00:03:47,045
A storm of particles that left the Sun
four days ago. Slower than light.

61
00:03:47,515 --> 00:03:50,300
The last letter the Sun will ever send.

62
00:03:50,770 --> 00:03:52,485
MIRA: It is saying goodbye.

63
00:03:56,755 --> 00:03:59,590
Day five. The sea starts to freeze.

64
00:04:00,060 --> 00:04:03,150
But only on top. The ice is a lid.

65
00:04:03,620 --> 00:04:06,830
Underneath, the ocean stays
liquid for years.

66
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:08,790
MIRA: The fish are fine!

67
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:14,460
Day seven. Minus eighteen. Every heater
on Earth is switched on at once.

68
00:04:14,930 --> 00:04:16,790
The grid cannot take it.

69
00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:19,695
Your building goes dark at midnight.

70
00:04:20,165 --> 00:04:22,655
An hour later, you smell smoke.

71
00:04:23,125 --> 00:04:26,890
Next door, someone is burning
their furniture. Indoors.

72
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:31,170
The smoke you can see. The
carbon monoxide, you cannot.

73
00:04:31,640 --> 00:04:33,405
You get everyone out.

74
00:04:33,875 --> 00:04:36,085
At the door, Biscuit panics.

75
00:04:36,555 --> 00:04:37,690
MIRA: BISCUIT!

76
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:40,875
You search all night.

77
00:04:41,345 --> 00:04:43,935
Then again, it is always night now.

78
00:04:46,605 --> 00:04:48,690
Paw prints. Fresh ones.

79
00:04:49,160 --> 00:04:52,170
They lead to the only warm
thing on the street.

80
00:04:52,640 --> 00:04:56,200
The metro. Warm air, rising
from the tunnels.

81
00:04:56,670 --> 00:05:00,705
A few metres down, the ground is
still around ten degrees.

82
00:05:01,175 --> 00:05:03,690
It is still holding on to last summer.

83
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:07,695
Biscuit did not run away. He
found the way down.

84
00:05:08,165 --> 00:05:11,825
By morning, half the city has
followed him underground.

85
00:05:12,295 --> 00:05:15,385
Day thirty. The metro is a city now.

86
00:05:15,855 --> 00:05:21,515
Lettuce grows under lamps in the old tunnels.
Everyone is already sick of lettuce.

87
00:05:21,985 --> 00:05:25,545
Every morning at seven, they
switch on the big lamps.

88
00:05:26,015 --> 00:05:27,930
People call it sunrise.

89
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:31,735
Mira teaches the little ones
how to draw the Sun.

90
00:05:32,205 --> 00:05:35,490
Some of them have already
forgotten what colour it was.

91
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:40,600
Biscuit, meanwhile, has a fan
club. And a bigger belly.

92
00:05:41,070 --> 00:05:42,885
MIRA: Are we going to run out of air?

93
00:05:43,355 --> 00:05:49,090
No. Even with no plants making more, the air
we have would last thousands of years.

94
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:53,800
Ito spends the month rebuilding a
firefighter’s heat camera.

95
00:05:54,270 --> 00:05:59,160
MR. ITO: If something is out there, it
has to lose heat. Everything does.

96
00:05:59,630 --> 00:06:03,140
Day forty. Noon. You climb out with Ito.

97
00:06:03,610 --> 00:06:06,375
He aims at the spot where
the Sun should be.

98
00:06:06,845 --> 00:06:08,360
MR. ITO: It's warm.

99
00:06:09,330 --> 00:06:12,845
And it is bigger than the
Sun. Much bigger.

100
00:06:13,315 --> 00:06:15,425
Not a cloud. Not a moon.

101
00:06:15,895 --> 00:06:18,235
A shell. Built around a star.

102
00:06:19,505 --> 00:06:20,790
MIRA: Who built it?

103
00:06:22,060 --> 00:06:25,145
Day one hundred. The power plant fails.

104
00:06:25,615 --> 00:06:29,330
Ito goes with the engineers.
He is seventy-four.

105
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:31,615
The tunnels get cold.

106
00:06:32,085 --> 00:06:35,645
For the first time, you wonder
if this is how it ends.

107
00:06:37,015 --> 00:06:39,730
Day one eighty. The lights come back.

108
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:44,635
Ito comes back covered in grease and
very pleased with himself.

109
00:06:45,105 --> 00:06:51,170
(RADIO) Submarine crews report the life around
deep sea vents is completely unaffected.

110
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:55,230
Down there, life runs on heat
from inside the Earth.

111
00:06:55,700 --> 00:06:57,735
It never needed the Sun.

112
00:06:58,205 --> 00:07:00,195
MR. ITO: Neither will we.

113
00:07:00,665 --> 00:07:04,455
Day three hundred. Ito's
camera is better now.

114
00:07:04,925 --> 00:07:09,860
A thin, warm thread is leaving the
shell. Dust, glowing in its path.

115
00:07:10,330 --> 00:07:12,895
MR. ITO: It is not blocking the Sun.

116
00:07:13,365 --> 00:07:15,230
It is taking it.

117
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:19,235
All of the Sun’s light,
sent somewhere else.

118
00:07:20,305 --> 00:07:23,840
Day three sixty-five. Minus seventy-three.

119
00:07:24,310 --> 00:07:26,795
The clearest sky in human history.

120
00:07:27,265 --> 00:07:30,779
MIRA: One whole lap around the
Sun. Happy birthday, Earth.

121
00:07:33,849 --> 00:07:37,784
Eight minutes later, the Sun
comes back. For one second.

122
00:07:41,654 --> 00:07:44,814
Then every radio on Earth
picks up a signal.

123
00:07:45,284 --> 00:07:47,969
It is coming from the
direction of the Sun.

124
00:07:48,439 --> 00:07:49,479
MR. ITO: One.

125
00:07:50,329 --> 00:07:51,369
Two.

126
00:07:52,219 --> 00:07:53,359
Three.

127
00:07:54,629 --> 00:07:57,194
It is counting the planets.

128
00:07:57,664 --> 00:07:59,404
And it stopped at three.

129
00:08:00,874 --> 00:08:02,082
MIRA: That's us.

130
00:08:03,752 --> 00:08:06,737
What would you say back? Tell
us in the comments.

131
00:08:07,087 --> 00:08:09,177
Part two. The reply.
